WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:04.210
Thank you Peter.

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:05.330
Welcome everyone.

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:11.140
I am joined by Sanjida Rangwala and Valerie
Schneider is on the phone.

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:19.980
She is the lead of the group who works on
the sequence viewers at NCBI.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:28.050
The idea is to advertise the fact that we
now have track hubs available in NCBI's data

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:29.050
viewer.

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:34.670
And give a demonstration of how to find track
hubs and connect them and see what they look

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:36.910
like in the displays.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:40.080
Today's topics will be what is a track hub?

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.250
Maybe some of you don't know.

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:46.990
I will give a very brief introduction to that.

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:53.750
And I will show you how you can search for
them within the Genome Data Viewer and a bit

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:57.340
on how to connect to those and display them.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:02.630
Then I will jump into the viewer into a live
demo, emphasizing some of the features.

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:09.210
I will wrap up with some general features
of the Genome Data Viewer such as how to Sheriff

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:13.729
your browser view once you have configured
it.

00:01:13.729 --> 00:01:14.859
What is a track hub?

00:01:14.859 --> 00:01:17.789
I put this reference up here for two reasons.

00:01:17.789 --> 00:01:20.820
It is a great source of background information.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:26.479
If you aren't familiar with them, this is
a good place.

00:01:26.479 --> 00:01:33.990
It reminds me that track hubs are not an NCBI
project.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:38.729
These were developed by the good folks at
UCSC.

00:01:38.729 --> 00:01:44.909
From a users point of view, the track hub
is a method of displaying annotation data.

00:01:44.909 --> 00:01:50.579
These data can be epigenomics, they can be
proteomics, they can be expression data, features

00:01:50.579 --> 00:01:53.969
like transcription start sites, that sort
of thing.

00:01:53.969 --> 00:02:00.299
These are typically pretty large files in
formats like bigBed and bigWig and BAM.

00:02:00.299 --> 00:02:05.249
The real advantages of track hubs is that
the data are on a remote server, relative

00:02:05.249 --> 00:02:15.300
to you, so you don't have to maintain the
data.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:26.120
Also, whenever you ask the browser to retrieve
a certain bit of data it only has to retrieve

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:30.690
what is being displayed in your browser at
the moment.

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:33.230
That range of genomic coordinates.

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:36.599
It does not have to pull everything that is
in the project.

00:02:36.599 --> 00:02:40.060
It only pulls what it needs to show in that
particular view.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:44.629
That makes loading the tracks pretty fast.

00:02:44.629 --> 00:02:49.409
As more and more track hubs have become available,
there needed to be a way to organize them

00:02:49.409 --> 00:02:51.810
and have a good search interface.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:56.320
So the Track Hub Registry came into being,
primarily from the folks at EMBL-EBI.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:05.010
I encourage you to look at this website, www.trackhubregistry.org
to get more background.

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:12.180
I good point of the Genome Data Viewer is
we provide a search interface so you can easily

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:17.099
search the Registry using free text.

00:03:17.099 --> 00:03:20.760
I'm going to start on the Genome Data Viewer
homepage.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:27.870
I've blown up the section on the right where
you can choose the assembly.

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:32.969
You do need to make sure you are working in
the correct assembly version for the annotation

00:03:32.969 --> 00:03:34.879
data you want to use.

00:03:34.879 --> 00:03:38.780
The data is of course mapped to a particular
version.

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:44.269
The track hub registry has many different
organisms and it often has more than one assembly

00:03:44.269 --> 00:03:46.079
for a given organism.

00:03:46.079 --> 00:03:50.129
Make sure you choose the correct assembly.

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:56.069
Once you do that you can either click on the
Browse Genome button or use the search box

00:03:56.069 --> 00:04:02.150
above to locate a particular chromosome or
region on a chromosome or gene or something

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:03.459
like that.

00:04:03.459 --> 00:04:10.780
Let's say you click on that browse genome
button, you will get a view like this.

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:14.650
On the left-hand side of the page are what
we call widgets.

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:20.830
There is a search widget and a blast widget,
and the one we'll be talking about today is

00:04:20.830 --> 00:04:23.670
the user data and track hubs widget.

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:26.580
I'll expand that a bit.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:29.170
Within it is an options menu.

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:32.550
You can see it expanded on the right.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:35.570
Under options is a selection for track hubs.

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:40.520
This is also where you could load up your
local files of user data.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:46.819
If you click on track hubs that will take
you into a track hub configuration menu, where

00:04:46.819 --> 00:04:53.980
you can start your search, and here you can
input the URL for the hub if you know it.

00:04:53.980 --> 00:05:00.080
You can also restrict by datatype, genomics,
transcriptomics, proteomics.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:06.060
In many cases just searching any of those
is fine.

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:10.080
Let's say I wanted hubs associated with the
Gencode project.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:18.310
I could simply type gencode in there, hit
Go, and on this day, that retrieved three

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:19.310
different hubs.

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:25.990
Keep in mind this is from the GRCh37 or hg19
assembly.

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:33.980
So you have gencode related data of different
types, long non-coding RNA RACE data, predicted

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:39.789
microRNA target sites, and a project called
PhyloCSF.

00:05:39.789 --> 00:05:45.229
Let's say I'm interested in that, and check
that box, it changes the display of the menu

00:05:45.229 --> 00:05:48.380
and lists all of the tracks in that hub.

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:52.960
I will share this in more detail when I get
into the live demo.

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:59.470
At the bottom of that list, it also shows
you other assemblies on which the track hub

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:00.470
is available.

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:05.580
It is difficult to tell on the slide, those
are grayed out because they would not be able

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:08.300
to display in this assembly.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:11.949
At least you know they are available.

00:06:11.949 --> 00:06:18.610
There is the other human assembly, the current
assembly, and then assemblies for mouse, chicken,

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:21.370
fruit fly, and C elegans.

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:26.620
When you select the tracks you want to load,
you just check a box for the ones that you

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:31.979
want, you get a nice green light on the right
when the tracks load up, and then you hit

00:06:31.979 --> 00:06:38.280
the configure button, and it adds those tracks
into the bottom of your view.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:43.680
Those should be displaying as green tinted
tracks, they are not on my monitor for some

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:44.680
reason.

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:50.139
And those two tracks at the bottom, the two
that I had requested, I have zoomed in to

00:06:50.139 --> 00:06:51.960
a particular gene.

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:57.590
If we want to look at that in more detail
we can zoom in farther, we see here that the

00:06:57.590 --> 00:07:03.250
donor predictions are lining up at one end
of an exon, and the splice acceptor predictions

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:04.250
at the other.

00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:05.700
That looks good.

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:08.659
That is all I had to do for the slides.

00:07:08.659 --> 00:07:14.569
I'm going to jump into the Genome Data Viewer
and do a live demo.

00:07:14.569 --> 00:07:19.449
Here I am on a browser in the Genome Data
Viewer.

00:07:19.449 --> 00:07:28.509
I want to back up a bit and give you perspective
on why we think using track hubs is important.

00:07:28.509 --> 00:07:34.110
Let me increase the zoom level on this page.

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:39.530
You see up here at the top, we have this nice
array of over 600 eukaryotic RefSeq genome

00:07:39.530 --> 00:07:42.410
assemblies and their annotation.

00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:48.139
As more people put data into track hubs, you
will be able to align this other source of

00:07:48.139 --> 00:07:52.130
data in the same viewer with the NCBI annotations.

00:07:52.130 --> 00:07:58.169
The goal is, having two or more different
sources of annotation, might be able to come

00:07:58.169 --> 00:08:03.030
up with some new insights or make better annotations.

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:05.160
That is the overall goal.

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:11.070
With that introduction, I will jump into the
Genome Data Viewer and see how the track hubs

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:12.970
can help facilitate that goal.

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:17.110
I am going to choose GRCh37.

00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:23.620
There are many more hubs available for GRCh38,
but I'm just using this as an example.

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:26.129
I'm going to click on Browse genome.

00:08:26.129 --> 00:08:33.090
That is loading the page.

00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:39.620
As always the viewer comes up with a default
set of tracks.

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:43.769
I am going to close a couple of these widgets
